  "worked_solution": "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\u2282{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={\u222c}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},$ and the event $A={X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\u222c}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & \u22480.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
